\subsection{Space Thermal Environment}

% Apoyo conceptual y de estructura tomado del TFG de Carolina, apartado 4.2.
Spacecraft thermal control can be understood as an energy-management problem in which the
external environment has a major influence on the thermal design. Unlike terrestrial systems,
space systems operate in vacuum, so external convection is practically nonexistent. Therefore,
radiation becomes the only mechanism by which the spacecraft can reject heat to its surroundings,
while internal heat transfer still occurs mainly by conduction through the structure and the
mechanical interfaces between components [2,3,4].

The thermal environment in space is especially demanding because it is highly variable and depends
on orbital altitude, inclination, beta angle, spacecraft attitude, and the thermo-optical properties
of the exposed surfaces. In Earth orbit, the main external heat sources are direct solar radiation,
planetary albedo, and terrestrial infrared radiation, while deep space acts as a cold sink with an
effective temperature close to $3\,\mathrm{K}$ [2,3,4].

For an external surface $i$, the absorbed direct solar heat load can be written as
\begin{equation}
    \dot{Q}_{\mathrm{sol},i} = \alpha_{s,i}\, G_s\, A_i \cos\theta_i
    \label{eq:qsol}
\end{equation}
where $\alpha_{s,i}$ is the solar absorptivity of the surface, $G_s$ is the solar irradiance,
$A_i$ is the exposed area, and $\theta_i$ is the angle between the solar vector and the outward
normal of the surface [3,4].

In a simplified form, the absorbed albedo contribution may be expressed as
\begin{equation}
    \dot{Q}_{\mathrm{alb},i} = \alpha_{s,i}\, G_{\mathrm{alb}}\, A_i\, F_{i\rightarrow E}
    \label{eq:qalb}
\end{equation}
where $G_{\mathrm{alb}}$ is an effective reflected solar irradiance from the Earth and
$F_{i\rightarrow E}$ is the view factor from the surface to the Earth.

Similarly, the absorbed planetary infrared load can be approximated as
\begin{equation}
    \dot{Q}_{\mathrm{IR},i} = \varepsilon_i\, G_{\mathrm{IR}}\, A_i\, F_{i\rightarrow E}
    \label{eq:qir}
\end{equation}
where $\varepsilon_i$ is the infrared emissivity of the surface and $G_{\mathrm{IR}}$ is the
effective Earth infrared flux [2,3].

If a surface radiates mainly toward deep space, the net radiative heat loss can be approximated by
\begin{equation}
    \dot{Q}_{\mathrm{rad},i} =
    \varepsilon_i \sigma A_i \left(T_i^4 - T_{\mathrm{ds}}^4\right)
    \label{eq:qrad_ds}
\end{equation}
where $\sigma$ is the Stefan--Boltzmann constant and $T_{\mathrm{ds}}$ is the effective deep-space
temperature [3,5].

At system level, the thermal state of the spacecraft can be interpreted as the result of the balance
between absorbed environmental fluxes, internally dissipated power, conductive redistribution, and
radiative emission. In a compact form, the global energy balance may be expressed as
\begin{equation}
    \dot{Q}_{\mathrm{int}} + \dot{Q}_{\mathrm{sol}} + \dot{Q}_{\mathrm{alb}} + \dot{Q}_{\mathrm{IR}}
    = \dot{Q}_{\mathrm{emit}}
    \label{eq:global_balance}
\end{equation}
where $\dot{Q}_{\mathrm{int}}$ represents the internally dissipated heat and
$\dot{Q}_{\mathrm{emit}}$ the heat radiated away by the spacecraft.

In practice, this environment produces strong thermal oscillations, especially in Low Earth Orbit
(LEO), where the spacecraft repeatedly alternates between sunlight and eclipse. These cyclic
variations may cause significant temperature excursions, thermal gradients, and structural
stresses if they are not properly managed by the thermal control system [2,3,4]. For that
reason, spacecraft thermal design requires a careful combination of geometry, materials,
surface coatings, thermal interfaces, insulation, and radiative surfaces in order to keep all
components within their operational and survival temperature limits.

\subsection{Heat Transfer Mechanisms}

% Apoyo conceptual y de estructura tomado del TFG de Carolina, apartado 4.3.
Heat transfer is the process by which thermal energy is exchanged between systems or between
different regions of the same system as a consequence of a temperature difference. In classical
heat-transfer theory, the three fundamental mechanisms are conduction, convection, and radiation
[5]. However, in spacecraft applications convection with the external environment is absent because
of the vacuum conditions of space. As a result, conduction and radiation become the dominant heat
transfer mechanisms [2,3,4].

In a spacecraft, conduction governs the redistribution of heat through structural panels, brackets,
interfaces, shelves, and thermal straps, while radiation controls both the exchange with the
external environment and the thermal interaction between exposed surfaces. Consequently, an
accurate thermal model must represent both mechanisms in a coupled way.

\subsubsection{Conduction Heat Transfer}

Heat transfer by conduction occurs within a material or between bodies in contact when a temperature
gradient exists. Microscopically, this phenomenon is associated with molecular interactions and,
in metallic materials, also with the transport of energy by free electrons [5,6]. The constitutive
equation governing conduction is Fourier's law:
\begin{equation}
    \vec{q} = -k \nabla T
    \label{eq:fourier_vec}
\end{equation}
where $\vec{q}$ is the heat flux vector and $k$ is the thermal conductivity of the medium.

For one-dimensional steady conduction through a plane wall of thickness $L$ and area $A$,
Eq.~(\ref{eq:fourier_vec}) reduces to
\begin{equation}
    \dot{Q} = -kA\frac{dT}{dx}
    \label{eq:fourier_1d_bis}
\end{equation}
and, if the thermal conductivity is constant, it can be integrated to give
\begin{equation}
    \dot{Q} = \frac{kA}{L}\left(T_1-T_2\right).
    \label{eq:flat_wall}
\end{equation}

This expression motivates the definition of conductive thermal resistance:
\begin{equation}
    R_{\mathrm{cond}}=\frac{L}{kA},
    \qquad
    \dot{Q}=\frac{T_1-T_2}{R_{\mathrm{cond}}}.
    \label{eq:rcond_bis}
\end{equation}

The thermal resistance concept is especially useful in nodal thermal models, since conductive
paths can be treated in direct analogy with electrical resistors. This representation is commonly
used in spacecraft thermal analysis to describe heat transfer through structural members, equipment
mountings, and thermal links [3,7].

For transient heat conduction, the temperature field is governed by the heat equation:
\begin{equation}
    \rho c_p \frac{\partial T}{\partial t}
    =
    \nabla \cdot (k \nabla T) + \dot{q}
    \label{eq:heat_equation_bis}
\end{equation}
where $\rho$ is the density, $c_p$ the specific heat capacity, and $\dot{q}$ the volumetric heat
generation term [5,6]. This equation constitutes the basis for more advanced discretization methods
used in thermal simulation.

In real spacecraft systems, conduction also takes place across mechanical interfaces. Because real
surfaces are rough and only contact at discrete micro-asperities, the thermal path between two
connected components is not ideal. This effect is represented through the thermal contact resistance,
which can be expressed as
\begin{equation}
    R_c = \frac{1}{h_c A}
    \label{eq:rcontact_bis}
\end{equation}
where $h_c$ is the contact conductance and $A$ is the apparent contact area [2,7]. This contribution
may be important in bolted joints, support brackets, electronic box mountings, or optical payload
interfaces, and should be taken into account when the thermal coupling between components is critical.

Another common conductive element in spacecraft thermal control is the thermal strap. Thermal straps
are flexible links, often made of copper, aluminium, or graphite-based materials, used to transport
heat efficiently between a hot component and a colder sink, such as a radiator or a structural panel
[2]. Their main advantage is that they provide high thermal conductance while accommodating geometric
constraints and differential expansion. For this reason, they are frequently employed in thermal
control systems involving sensitive payloads or components with localized dissipations.

\subsubsection{Radiation Heat Transfer}

Thermal radiation is the transfer of energy by electromagnetic waves emitted by matter at temperatures
above absolute zero. Unlike conduction and convection, radiation does not require a material medium
and can propagate through vacuum. This makes it the dominant mechanism by which spacecraft exchange
heat with their external environment [2,5].

The energy associated with radiation may be described through Planck's relation,
\begin{equation}
    E = h\nu
    \label{eq:planck}
\end{equation}
where $h$ is Planck's constant and $\nu$ is the frequency of the emitted radiation. Since frequency
and wavelength are related by
\begin{equation}
    \lambda = \frac{c}{\nu},
    \label{eq:lambda_relation}
\end{equation}
thermal radiation has both spectral and directional dependence.

The ideal radiating body is the blackbody, which absorbs all incident radiation and emits the maximum
possible energy at a given temperature. Its total emissive power is given by the Stefan--Boltzmann
law:
\begin{equation}
    E_b = \sigma T^4
    \label{eq:stefan_boltzmann_bis}
\end{equation}
where $\sigma$ is the Stefan--Boltzmann constant.

For a real surface, the emitted power is reduced by the emissivity:
\begin{equation}
    E = \varepsilon \sigma T^4
    \label{eq:real_surface_emission}
\end{equation}
where $\varepsilon$ is the emissivity of the material. If the surface exchanges radiation with a large
isothermal environment, the net radiative heat exchange may be approximated as
\begin{equation}
    \dot{Q}_{\mathrm{rad}} =
    \varepsilon \sigma A \left(T_s^4 - T_{\mathrm{sur}}^4\right)
    \label{eq:qrad_net}
\end{equation}
where $A$ is the emitting area, $T_s$ is the surface temperature and $T_{\mathrm{sur}}$ is the
surrounding temperature [3,5].

In practical spacecraft analysis, surfaces are usually treated as diffuse-gray, so their radiative
behaviour can be described using radiosity and irradiation. For an opaque diffuse-gray surface, the
radiosity is
\begin{equation}
    J = \varepsilon \sigma T^4 + (1-\varepsilon)G
    \label{eq:radiosity}
\end{equation}
where $G$ is the irradiation incident on the surface.

The geometric coupling between surfaces is represented by the view factor $F_{ij}$, defined as the
fraction of radiation leaving surface $i$ that directly reaches surface $j$. These factors satisfy
the reciprocity relation
\begin{equation}
    A_i F_{ij} = A_j F_{ji}
    \label{eq:reciprocity_bis}
\end{equation}
and the enclosure relation
\begin{equation}
    \sum_{j=1}^{N} F_{ij}=1.
    \label{eq:closure_bis}
\end{equation}

For two diffuse-gray surfaces, the net radiative exchange can be written in resistance-network form as
\begin{equation}
    \dot{Q}_{12} =
    \frac{\sigma \left(T_1^4-T_2^4\right)}
    {
        \dfrac{1-\varepsilon_1}{A_1\varepsilon_1}
        +
        \dfrac{1}{A_1F_{12}}
        +
        \dfrac{1-\varepsilon_2}{A_2\varepsilon_2}
    }.
    \label{eq:q12_gray_bis}
\end{equation}

This expression is particularly relevant in spacecraft thermal design because radiative behaviour
depends strongly on thermo-optical properties. Surface finishes and coatings therefore become one
of the main design variables of the thermal control system. In general, black paints tend to provide
high solar absorptivity and high emissivity, white paints reduce solar absorptivity while maintaining
high emissivity, polished metals usually exhibit low absorptivity and low emissivity, and second
surface mirrors are typically used when low absorptivity and high infrared emissivity are desired
[2,3,4]. These properties determine whether a surface is better suited to absorb heat, reject heat,
or minimize environmental heating.

\subsection{Mathematical Modeling of Thermal Systems}

% Apoyo conceptual y de estructura tomado del TFG de Carolina, apartado 4.4.
Mathematical modeling is essential to predict the thermal behaviour of space systems and to design
effective thermal control strategies before hardware is manufactured or tested. Since a real
spacecraft is a distributed physical system with complex geometry, multiple heat paths, variable
external loads and internal dissipations, analytical solutions are only feasible for very simplified
cases. For realistic applications, numerical methods are required [2,3,7].

In general terms, the thermal model is obtained by applying energy conservation to a discretized
representation of the system. Each discrete element stores thermal energy, exchanges heat with
neighbouring elements by conduction and radiation, receives external environmental loads, and may
also include internal power dissipation. Depending on the desired fidelity and computational cost,
this discretization can be approached through high-resolution spatial methods, such as the Finite
Element Method (FEM), or through reduced-order nodal methods, such as the Lumped Parameter Method
(LPM), which is the basis of many spacecraft thermal network solvers [7].

\subsubsection{Disadvantages of the Finite Element Method (FEM)}

The Finite Element Method is a powerful tool for solving thermal problems in complex geometries with
high spatial detail. Nevertheless, for spacecraft thermal design at system level it presents several
limitations.

First, FEM models usually require a detailed geometric description and a fine mesh in order to
capture local gradients accurately. This increases the number of degrees of freedom, the model
preparation effort, and the computational cost. In spacecraft thermal analysis, where many
steady-state and transient cases must be evaluated under different boundary conditions, orbital
configurations, attitudes and dissipation maps, this can become inefficient [5,7].

Second, spacecraft thermal analysis is not only a conduction problem. It also involves radiative
exchange, orbital heating, thermo-optical properties, contact resistances, MLI behaviour, boundary
conditions, and subsystem-level dissipations. Although FEM can represent many of these phenomena,
dedicated thermal network tools often provide more direct and flexible ways to include them in
engineering models [2,3,7].

Third, in system-level thermal design the analyst is often more interested in component temperatures,
heat-flow paths, and subsystem balances than in very local temperature fields. Therefore, the level
of spatial detail offered by FEM does not always translate into a practical advantage during
preliminary or iterative thermal design phases.

Finally, FEM output can be more cumbersome to interpret when the objective is to understand and
visualize thermal interactions between subsystems. Since the focus of this work is precisely the
post-processing and graphical analysis of nodal heat flows exported from ESATAN-TMS, a network-based
approach is more naturally aligned with the purpose of the thesis.

This does not mean that FEM is not useful for thermal analysis. On the contrary, it is especially
valuable when local gradients, detailed structural coupling, or component-level verification are of
interest. However, for spacecraft thermal analysis at engineering level, a reduced-order nodal method
is often preferable.

\subsubsection{Lumped Parameter Method (LPM)}

The Lumped Parameter Method is a discretization technique in which a continuous thermal system is
represented by a finite set of isothermal nodes connected through conductive and radiative couplings.
Each node represents a component, a portion of structure, a surface patch, or any region for which
the assumption of uniform temperature is acceptable [7].

The key assumption of the method is that the temperature gradients inside each node are negligible
compared with the gradients between nodes. Under this hypothesis, each node can be described by a
single temperature $T_i(t)$ and a thermal capacitance
\begin{equation}
    C_i = m_i c_{p,i}
    \label{eq:capacitance_bis}
\end{equation}
where $m_i$ is the nodal mass and $c_{p,i}$ is the specific heat capacity.

Applying energy conservation to node $i$, the transient nodal energy balance can be written as
\begin{equation}
    C_i \frac{dT_i}{dt}
    =
    Q_i
    -
    \sum_{j=1}^{n} G^{L}_{ij}(T_i-T_j)
    -
    \sum_{j=1}^{n} G^{R}_{ij}(T_i^4-T_j^4)
    \label{eq:lpm_balance_bis}
\end{equation}
where:
\begin{itemize}
    \item $C_i$ is the thermal capacitance of node $i$,
    \item $Q_i$ includes internal dissipation and external thermal loads,
    \item $G^{L}_{ij}$ is the linear conductive conductance between nodes $i$ and $j$,
    \item $G^{R}_{ij}$ is the radiative conductance factor between nodes $i$ and $j$.
\end{itemize}

The conductive exchange between two nodes may be expressed as
\begin{equation}
    \dot{Q}^{\mathrm{cond}}_{ij} = G^{L}_{ij}(T_j-T_i),
    \qquad
    G^{L}_{ij} = \frac{1}{R_{ij}}
    \label{eq:gl_bis}
\end{equation}
while the radiative exchange is naturally nonlinear because of the fourth-power temperature
dependence. In some applications it can be linearized around a mean temperature $T_m$, leading to
an equivalent radiative conductance proportional to $4\sigma T_m^3$.

The LPM is particularly suitable for spacecraft thermal analysis because it offers an excellent
balance between physical fidelity, interpretability, and computational efficiency. It allows the
analyst to represent spacecraft subsystems directly as engineering nodes, to combine conductive and
radiative couplings in a single framework, and to perform long transient simulations with moderate
computational cost [3,7].

Furthermore, the nodal nature of the method makes it especially convenient for post-processing.
Since the outputs are already organised as node temperatures, conductors, and exchanged heat flows,
they can be grouped and visualized directly at subsystem level. This is particularly useful in the
context of the present work, where the main objective is to develop a Python-based tool for the
graphical post-processing of ESATAN-TMS thermal results.

In practice, the accuracy of an LPM model depends strongly on the chosen discretization. If too
few nodes are used, relevant gradients or couplings may be lost; if too many are introduced, the
model becomes unnecessarily complex and harder to maintain. Therefore, the nodal representation must
always be selected as a compromise between physical realism, computational efficiency, and the
engineering objectives of the analysis [7].

